```

So `A` must be:

- **exported** (capital `A`) — a lowercase `a` is invisible to the external
  `suite_test` package and keeps the build red;
- a **function**, callable as `A()` with **zero arguments** — not a variable,
  constant, or method;
- returning **exactly one value** that is comparable to the untyped constant
  `1`. Use the default `int` return type. A `float64`/`int64`/custom numeric
  type would still compile against `!= 1`, but `int` is the plainest reading of
  the contract and avoids surprising any later caller;
- deterministic, with **no parameters and no state**: every call returns the
  same value.

**Intended implementation:** a trivial pure accessor whose body returns the
value `1`. There is no hidden arithmetic to reconstruct here — the suite pins a
single constant result, so the honest implementation is the constant. Do not
invent configuration, flags, randomness, time dependence, globals that could be
mutated, or an `init()` that computes the value; any of those add ways for the
value to drift away from `1` without buying anything. Do not make `A` return an
error or a second value — the call site uses it in a single-value expression
context and extra results will not compile.

**Serves:** `TestA` in `tests/suite/calc_test.go`, and (via compilation of the
reference `calc.A`) the `<build>` pseudo-test that is the nominal subject of
